{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`rebar create` today warns only when a new title normalizes byte-identically to a recent one: `duplicate_create_warning` (`src/rebar/_commands/recent_creates.py:117`), gated by `verify.suggest_duplicate_tickets` (`:129`), matching on `title_norm` equality (`:103`). ADR 0122 measured that stratum at 1,431 of 1,524 recorded duplicate pairs. The remaining 93 pairs differ in wording and are invisible to equality matching; they are why agents re-file work that already has a ticket.\n\nThis story builds the complete user-visible feature on a retrieval tier that needs no model provider, no credentials and no network, so it is available in every installation. The sibling embedding tier is a later, opt-in ranking upgrade over the same index, catch-up and surfacing machinery built here.\n\n## Approach\n\n### Retrieval\n\nsqlite FTS5 over title and description with bm25 weights title 5.0 / description 1.0 — the configuration ADR 0122 measured as the `fts5` baseline (`evals/similar-tickets/measure.py:389-396`, weights `\"0.0, 5.0, 1.0\"`). It reaches semantic recall@5 0.823 against the adopted `vector` candidate's 0.876, and exceeds `vector` at lexical recall@10 (0.993 vs 0.972, p=0.0000), which ADR 0122 records as the reason it is not removed. Query is the new ticket's title plus the first 200 characters of its description, per ADR 0122's architecture section.\n\nThe query ticket's own id is filtered from the candidate set. Catch-up indexes the just-created ticket and the query is that ticket's own text, so without an explicit filter a create returns itself as its top match and spends one of the 5 slots on it.\n\nResults are capped at 5. ADR 0122's decision is scoped to k=5: at k=10 `vector`'s semantic edge is indistinguishable (p=0.3438) while `fts5`'s lexical edge becomes significant, an operating point at which the evidence favours building nothing. Surfacing a different count invalidates the ADR's comparison and would require re-running `measure.py --paired`.\n\n`score` is a relevance value normalized to the closed interval 0 to 1, where 1 is the most relevant. sqlite's raw bm25 is negative and orders lower-is-better, which inverts the conventional reading of a score and cannot be asserted on meaningfully by a parity test. The normalization and its direction are documented in `create_result.schema.json` alongside the field's type.\n\n### Index and freshness\n\nA sqlite file under `.rebar/cache/similar-tickets/`, mirroring the git-ignored derived cache `.rebar/cache/plan-review/` already uses (`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/checkpoints.py:33-37`). `.gitignore:23` ignores `.rebar/*` wholesale, so no ignore change is needed and nothing derived can reach the `tickets` branch by construction.\n\nThe index persists a map from ticket directory to its last-indexed dir hash. That map is what makes catch-up cheap and what makes newly created tickets discoverable; both properties come from the map rather than from the hash function alone.\n\n`compute_dir_hash(ticket_dir, event_filenames)` (`src/rebar/reducer/_cache.py:122`) supplies the per-directory hash only. Its signature is scoped to a single ticket directory and its event filenames, so it cannot detect that a whole new ticket directory has appeared — it is a change detector for a known directory, not a discovery mechanism. Catch-up therefore runs in two steps:\n\n1. **Enumerate** the live ticket-directory set from the store.\n2. **Diff** that set against the persisted map. A directory with no entry is new and is indexed. A directory whose `compute_dir_hash` differs from its stored value is re-indexed. An entry whose directory is gone is dropped.\n\nStep 1 is what makes a ticket created moments earlier discoverable by the next create; without it, catch-up would only ever re-hash directories it already knew and a brand-new ticket would never be indexed.\n\n### Warm-path cost\n\nOn an unchanged store, step 2 performs no indexing work and no FTS5 writes: every enumerated directory's hash matches its stored value. The warm cost is therefore one directory enumeration plus one stat per event file, with no parse, no tokenization and no write. That is the cost the 500 ms p95 criterion is asserted against, and the criterion that no re-indexing occurs on an unchanged store is stated separately so the warm path is verified rather than assumed.\n\n### Concurrent writers\n\nThe CLI, the MCP server, the library facade and `rebar idea` can each run a create against the same index file at once, so two processes can both observe a changed hash and race to rebuild. Reconciliation writes are made atomic by building into a temporary database in the same directory and replacing the live file with `os.rename`, which is atomic within a filesystem. A reader that opens a corrupt or partial index discards it and triggers a full rebuild rather than returning an error, so the cache is regenerable at any time and needs no separate eviction path. Concurrent reconcilers converge because each recomputes the dir hashes it writes, so a loser's work is redone rather than merged.\n\n### Surfacing\n\nThe advisory seam already carries `duplicate_warning` and `description_warning` on the create result, and it spans more sites than `create` alone. `_lib_writes.py` builds its return dicts by hand-copying named advisory keys at `:172`, `:179`, `:224` and `:231`; `_commands/composer.py:463-467` re-emits them on CLI stderr so stdout stays pure in both output modes; `_commands/idea.py:93` does the same for `rebar idea`. Because every one of those sites names its keys individually, a new advisory key added only to `create` is silently dropped from `idea()`'s return dict and never reaches the idea CLI.\n\nAdvisory pass-through is therefore made generic at the `create_core` boundary so advisory keys propagate without a per-site edit, and the `idea` sites are covered by criteria of their own. Fixing this generically rather than by adding one more hand-copied key is what stops the next advisory from reproducing the same gap.\n\n`src/rebar/schemas/create_result.schema.json` already documents the advisory contract and is extended with `similar_tickets`.\n\n### Failure disposition\n\nThe advisory is computed after the write succeeds, and every failure path yields an empty field, following the bare `except Exception` at `recent_creates.py:150` that already makes the duplicate advisory incapable of failing a completed write. Each swallowed failure is logged at warning level before the empty advisory is returned. Without that log a corrupt index degrades the feature to permanently empty results with no observable symptom, which is indistinguishable from a store that genuinely has no similar tickets.\n\n### ADR amendments\n\nADR 0122 is amended by this story on three points, each of which its current text gets wrong once a lexical tier exists:\n\n- It records the tiering decision — lexical as the zero-provider default, embedding as the opt-in upgrade — which the ADR does not currently contain.\n- Its \"Deployment precondition\" section states the index cannot \"be built anywhere but a developer's own credentials\". That is false for the lexical tier, and the precondition is narrowed to the embedding tier.\n- It reports no latency figure anywhere. The epic requires the ADR to report the measured cold-catch-up p95, so the figure measured by this story is recorded there.\n\n### Gating and ratchets\n\nThe gate is `verify.suggest_duplicate_tickets`, the key that already gates create-time duplicate advice; this is the same advice with ranked results, so a second key would split one user-facing behaviour across two switches. No new config key is added. Any ratcheted mechanism the implementation does introduce carries a `# mechanism-ok:` marker at its definition site naming this ticket.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `rebar create --output json` returns a `similar_tickets` array of at most 5 entries. Checked by: a CLI test asserting the field's presence and length bound.\n- [ ] The library `create_ticket` return dict carries `similar_tickets`. Checked by: a library test asserting the key is present.\n- [ ] MCP `create_ticket` output carries `similar_tickets`. Checked by: an MCP test asserting the key is present in the tool result.\n- [ ] The library `idea()` return dict carries `similar_tickets`. Checked by: a library test asserting the key is present on an idea create.\n- [ ] The `rebar idea` CLI surfaces `similar_tickets` on stderr. Checked by: a CLI test capturing stderr on an idea create.\n- [ ] Each `similar_tickets` entry carries `id`, `alias`, `title` and `score`. Checked by: a shape test over one entry.\n- [ ] Every `score` lies in the closed interval 0 to 1 with 1 most relevant. Checked by: a test asserting the range and asserting the top-ranked entry's score is the maximum of the returned set.\n- [ ] `create_result.schema.json` documents `similar_tickets` including the `score` range and direction. Checked by: a schema test validating a sample result against the committed schema.\n- [ ] A created ticket's own id never appears in its own `similar_tickets` results. Checked by: a test creating a ticket whose text matches itself strongly and asserting its id is absent.\n- [ ] Catch-up enumerates the live ticket-directory set on each run, so a ticket directory created after the last index build is indexed. Checked by: a test that creates a ticket directory, runs catch-up, and asserts the ticket is returned by a subsequent query.\n- [ ] Catch-up drops index entries whose ticket directory no longer exists. Checked by: a test removing a directory and asserting the ticket stops being returned.\n- [ ] Catch-up performs no re-indexing work on an unchanged store. Checked by: a test asserting the count of re-indexed tickets is 0 on a second consecutive run.\n- [ ] Warm-index create-time retrieval completes within a p95 of 500 ms over at least 20 runs against a synthetic store the test generates and asserts to hold at least 10,000 tickets. Checked by: a timing test that asserts the generated store's ticket count before measuring.\n- [ ] A ticket created on a clone is returned as a candidate by the next create on that same clone, in one process, with no daemon, background thread, MCP server or CI involved. Checked by: an integration test performing both creates in-process.\n- [ ] The measured cold-catch-up p95 is recorded in `docs/adr/0122-similar-ticket-retrieval.md`. Checked by: the ADR containing the figure.\n- [ ] `docs/adr/0122-similar-ticket-retrieval.md` records the lexical-default tiering decision. Checked by: the ADR containing it.\n- [ ] `docs/adr/0122-similar-ticket-retrieval.md`'s deployment precondition is scoped to the embedding tier rather than to the index as a whole. Checked by: the ADR text no longer asserting the index requires credentials.\n- [ ] After 100 creates and 100 reads against a test store, the `tickets` branch contains only user event types. Checked by: an integration test asserting the branch's event-type set after the run.\n- [ ] With every model-provider credential environment variable unset, a create returns a non-empty ranked `similar_tickets` advisory. Checked by: an integration test that clears the credential environment.\n- [ ] A create exits 0 with an empty `similar_tickets` advisory when the index file is absent. Checked by: a test asserting exit status and advisory contents.\n- [ ] A create exits 0 when the index file is corrupt, and the corrupt index is replaced by a rebuild. Checked by: a test corrupting the file then asserting exit status and that a subsequent query returns results.\n- [ ] A create exits 0 with an empty `similar_tickets` advisory when catch-up raises. Checked by: a test injecting a raising catch-up and asserting exit status and advisory contents.\n- [ ] Each swallowed retrieval failure emits a warning-level log record. Checked by: a test capturing logs across the absent, corrupt and raising cases and asserting a record for each.\n- [ ] Two creates running concurrently against one index leave it readable and leave both tickets indexed. Checked by: a concurrency test running two creates in parallel then querying for both.\n- [ ] Every module added or modified under `src/rebar/` is at most 800 lines. Checked by: the CI module-size gate.\n- [ ] `python scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py --check` exits 0. Checked by: running it.\n- [ ] `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check` exits 0. Checked by: running it."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1793579858734648725,"uuid":"fb2b4e91-59e8-4ccc-8ab6-717f9a8d39e1"}